# `!(...)`: the fix only replaces `!` with `not`.
mode: syntax
---- query
from 'orders.csv' | where !(amount > 10 or amount < 1)
---- expected
PROGRAM@0..55
  PIPELINE@0..54
    IDENT@0..4 "from"
    WHITESPACE@4..5 " "
    LITERAL@5..17
      STRING@5..17 "'orders.csv'"
    WHITESPACE@17..18 " "
    PIPE@18..19 "|"
    WHITESPACE@19..20 " "
    WHERE_OP@20..54
      IDENT@20..25 "where"
      WHITESPACE@25..26 " "
      PREFIX_EXPR@26..54
        BANG@26..27 "!"
        PAREN_EXPR@27..54
          L_PAREN@27..28 "("
          BIN_EXPR@28..53
            BIN_EXPR@28..39
              NAME_REF@28..34
                IDENT@28..34 "amount"
              WHITESPACE@34..35 " "
              GT@35..36 ">"
              WHITESPACE@36..37 " "
              LITERAL@37..39
                LONG@37..39 "10"
            WHITESPACE@39..40 " "
            OR_KW@40..42 "or"
            WHITESPACE@42..43 " "
            BIN_EXPR@43..53
              NAME_REF@43..49
                IDENT@43..49 "amount"
              WHITESPACE@49..50 " "
              LT@50..51 "<"
              WHITESPACE@51..52 " "
              LITERAL@52..53
                LONG@52..53 "1"
          R_PAREN@53..54 ")"
  WHITESPACE@54..55 "\n"

error[DS0111]: `!` is not an operator
  --> query:1:27
  |
1 | from 'orders.csv' | where !(amount > 10 or amount < 1)
  |                           ^ this is C's "not"
  |
  = help: negate it with `not(...)`: `not(amount > 10 or amount < 1)`

with the machine-applicable fixes applied:
    from 'orders.csv' | where not(amount > 10 or amount < 1)
which parses without errors
